# Microsoft Zero Trust Assessment pack

The [Zero Trust Assessment](https://aka.ms/zerotrust/assessment) is Microsoft's free tool that checks a tenant
against Microsoft's Zero Trust guidance, across identity, devices, network, data, AI and security operations, and
says how to fix what it finds.
[Try the demo report](https://aka.ms/zerotrust/assessment/demo) to see what it covers.

This pack runs the assessment from Maester and shows each of its results in the Maester report, next to Maester's
own checks. It ships with Maester and runs only when you ask for it.

Each result is one row, `ZTA.<assessment test ID>` (for example `ZTA.21770`), with the assessment's title, risk
level (as the severity), description, remediation steps and findings. Results are tagged `ZeroTrust` and
`ZeroTrust.<pillar>` (`ZeroTrust.Identity`, `ZeroTrust.Devices` and so on).

## Report an issue with a test

Microsoft writes and maintains the assessment's tests. If a test's result, text or remediation looks wrong, raise
it in [microsoft/zerotrustassessment](https://github.com/microsoft/zerotrustassessment/issues). Problems with
running the pack from Maester go to [maester365/maester](https://github.com/maester365/maester/issues).

## Run it

Install the assessment's module once, connect Maester to the tenant, then name the pack:

```powershell
Install-Module ZeroTrustAssessment -Scope CurrentUser
Connect-Maester
Invoke-Maester -Pack ZTA -SkipBuiltIn    # only the Zero Trust Assessment
Invoke-Maester -Pack ZTA                 # Maester's tests and the assessment
```

function Get-MtBuiltInPack {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
    Returns the packs that ship with Maester: every folder under packs/ that holds a maester-pack.json.

    .DESCRIPTION
    In the built module the packs are in packs/ next to Maester.psd1; in a source checkout, in the
    repository's packs/ folder. They are part of the module, so they are trusted as the module is: they need
    no lock entry and no approval, and their files are checked against the module rather than a digest.
    Gallery modules they declare in Uses.Modules are still saved and pinned in the lock file, like any pack's
    (Install-MtPack <Id>).

    Each row has Id, Root, Manifest and Errors (from Read-MtPackManifest). Cached for the session.
    #>
    [CmdletBinding()]
    [OutputType([pscustomobject], [object[]])]
    param(
        # Read the folders again.
        [Parameter()]
        [switch] $Refresh
    )

    if ($null -ne $script:__MtBuiltInPacks -and -not $Refresh) { return $script:__MtBuiltInPacks }
    $moduleBase = $ExecutionContext.SessionState.Module.ModuleBase
    $root = @((Join-Path $moduleBase 'packs'), (Join-Path $moduleBase '../packs')) | Where-Object { Test-Path -LiteralPath $_ -PathType Container } | Select-Object -First 1
    $packs = @()
    if ($root) {
        $packs = @(foreach ($folder in (Get-ChildItem -LiteralPath $root -Directory | Sort-Object Name)) {
                if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath (Join-Path $folder.FullName 'maester-pack.json') -PathType Leaf)) { continue }
                $read = Read-MtPackManifest -Path $folder.FullName -Official
                [pscustomobject]@{
                    Id       = if ($read.Manifest) { [string]$read.Manifest.Id } else { $folder.Name }
                    Root     = $folder.FullName
                    Manifest = $read.Manifest
                    Errors   = $read.Errors
                }
            })
    }
    $script:__MtBuiltInPacks = $packs
    $script:__MtBuiltInPacks
}
